  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 225 PM CDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\nA weak wave of low pressure over sw Ontario will continue ewd\nacross nrn Lake Superior this evening along with an area of weak\nPVA via vorticity maximums. A little farther to the south, persistent\n850-700 mb warm, moist advection will continue across nrn MN to \nover Lake Superior this afternoon then gradually shift swd into \nUpper MI and nrn WI this evening via wly 850 mb flow of 25-30 kt.\n\nA tongue of projected RAP MLCAPE is expected to rise to 3000-3500\nJ/KG from wrn WI to just and west of Duluth by late this afternoon\nwith a high CIN region over ne MN due to cloud cover, then greater\ninstability with less CIN in sw Ontario. The CAMs due initiate \nconvection over ne MN this afternoon within this high CIN region,\nbut it is possible initiation could occur farther to the south\nwhere a weak boundary, from the passing sfc trough to the north, \nwill separate light wly winds from sswly winds to its south. It's\nalso possible the showers and scattered storms over nw MN will \ngrow upscale when encountering the higher CAPE environment to its \nse later this afternoon and evening. \n\nOverall there is much uncertainty on the timing and location this\nafternoon and early evening, but will count on the aforementioned\n850-700 mb warm, moist advection that will gradually shift swd \nacross nrn and central WI through the evening and overnight to \nbring some clarity to the forecast. MUCAPE should range from\n1500-3000 J/KG from east to west across the forecast area tnt,\nwhile the mid level cap likely stays over wrn WI. Some CAMs show \npersistent north to south showers and storms from late evening \ninto Mon AM which agrees well with the persistent warm, moist \nadvection via wly flow aloft. High PWATs of 1.5-1.8 inches will be\navailable and will need to message heavy rain and urban and small\nstream flooding. The recent dry conditions and the overall uncertainty\nprecludes the issuance of a Flood Watch at this time. \n\nAn Enhanced Risk is outlooked for WI but is counting on a mature\nand severe MCS. Deep layer nwly shear of 50 kt is in place along\nwith the aforementioned values of CAPE and mdt to large DCAPE.\nHowever, if more discrete cells or clusters of cells occur \ninstead of a large dominant MCS, large hail and damaging wet \nmicrobursts will still remain fairly probable just with lesser\ncoverage. The tornado threat looks low given relatively weak low \nlevel wind shear. \n\nFor Monday low pressure will track from east central MN across\ncentral or srn WI, while a shortwave trough tracks from sw \nOntario into the nrn Great Lakes. Scattered showers and storms may\nstill be ongoing Mon AM for the overnight hours, but should \nmostly dissipate by late morning. New shower and storm development\nis expected but will focus over the ern half of the forecast \narea. Believe temps will still rebound back into the 80s but there\nis much uncertainty given the clouds and precipitation. This will\nalso affect the potential for severe storms which does look a bit\nlower at this time. \n\nAfterward, this low pressure area and upper wave will occlude\nand remain over portions of the Mid Atlantic States and New\nEngland for Wed and the remainder of the week. This maintains\nnwly flow aloft for the wrn Great Lakes with a sfc ridge\nresiding over the area for Tue nt-Thu. Shortwave troughs in the\nflow aloft will then bring 40-50 percent chances for showers and \nstorms Thu nt-Sat. High temps in the lower to middle 80s are \nexpected for much of this period.",
